  WHAT THIS REPORT EVIDENCES
    The test source imports javax.persistence.PersistenceException at line 58.
    This is the SECOND report in this module archive carrying that import, and
    it is therefore direct baseline evidence, in TEST scope, for the persistence
    API resolution decision: advance the javax-namespace persistence API pin
    from 2.1.0 to 2.2.1, because the advanced provider implements the 2.2 API,
    and exclude the twin artifact the advanced provider pulls transitively under
    a different artifactId. Jar inspection decided it - the twin holds 209
    classes under javax/ and zero under the other prefix, and its class set is
    IDENTICAL to the javax-named artifact, so carrying both would put 211
    duplicate javax/persistence classes on the classpath under order-sensitive,
    non-reproducible resolution. The javax-named 2.2.1 artifact adds exactly two
    annotation container types over 2.1.0 and NEITHER appears in the source, so
    the advance carries ZERO source impact and ZERO effect on the schema
    generated from the persistence mappings. The exclusion is added at the
    single managed entry that introduces the twin, an entry that already
    carries an exclusions block, so the structure exists. Observed behaviour at
    the base commit was the tie-breaker that selected this resolution over the
    easier path of simply accepting the transitive twin. Under R-T1 the javax
    strings in this file are CORRECT and must be preserved verbatim.
    Behaviourally the two tests retrieve complaints FOR A USER, so this is
    user-scoped listing - the behaviour the case list, detail and document views
    smoke flow exercises. That flow's expected result depends on
    permission-evaluation outcomes being preserved, and they are: the permission
    evaluator and its supporting reflection scanning are unchanged. The
    reflection-scanning library was a textbook candidate blocker because it reads
    raw class bytes, and it is thoroughly load-bearing - seven or more main
    source classes across six modules depend on it - so it was EXECUTED against
    a modern class file rather than bumped on suspicion. The scan succeeded on
    the installed version with its existing companions, and its bytecode-reading
    companion parses modern class files across every release tested, because
    that reader never enforced a maximum class file version. The library is
    therefore left unchanged, which is itself a rule-compliance outcome: a
    version change without a demonstrated compatibility reason is out of scope.
    As a web-tier controller test it is equally baseline evidence for the
    preserved REST API contract - paths, HTTP verbs, request and response shapes
    and authentication behaviour are all held constant. It runs under the Spring
    JUnit 4 runner, so the framework advances only within its existing 5.3 line,
    from 5.3.2 to 5.3.39, with the security modules from 5.4.2 to 5.8.16, and
    there is no move to a newer framework generation and no
    application-framework starter.
    The class extends the mocking support base class, so it also exercises the
    mocking library whose version advances from 4.1 to 5.6.0 - the
    widest-footprint test-library change in the migration. Both 4.1 and the
    intermediate 4.3 fail identically on Java 17 with an inaccessible-object
    error raised from that library's class proxy factory; 5.6.0 succeeds, and
    the resolution was to UPGRADE THE LIBRARY rather than relax the JDK module
    boundaries.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A FAILURE
    Six integration-test classes in this module produce no report at all,
    because the unit-test phase stops before the integration-test goals bind.
    Two of them are the persistence-layer tests and one is the pipeline test,
    so the persistence layer is NOT represented in this archive and this
    unit-scope report is not a substitute for it. Three further classes in the
    module declare no test method and produce nothing. An absent integration
    test is NEITHER a pass NOR a failure, and it may NEVER justify a test
    exclusion under R-5. The same holds for the pre-existing ignored
    integration-test classes elsewhere in this plugin family: none of them is
    representable in this archive, and their absence is not a failure.
    This module contains no ignored test method of its own, which is why no
    skipped element appears below.

  NOT BYTE-COMPARABLE
    Unlike the five frontend build artefacts, this archive is not judged by
